#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054429 :question/id #uuid "e557c534-7392-5c31-9e08-43347736716e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13138" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb N, \\quad \\mathbb Z, \\quad \\mathbb Q, \\quad \\mathbb R, \\quad \\mathbb C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $3.25$ belong?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following: \n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Z}$ nor $\\mathbb{N},$  so we can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\notin\\mathbb N, \\qquad 3.25\\notin\\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- However, notice that $3.25$ can be written as the ratio of two integers as follows:\n\n$$\n3.25=\\dfrac{325}{100}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{Q}.\n$$\n\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb{C},$ and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad 3.25 \\in \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbb Q, \\mathbb R,$ and $\\mathbb C$ only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3de9e532-559c-5fc1-a6ad-5fcd1ed77824" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065 :topic/id #uuid "d7019ce6-160a-5aa4-9e08-78782f485dfa" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054851}] :topic/math-academy-id 45 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db